{"name":"Ocean Current","hex":"#537783","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"ocean current","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any more or less permanent or continuous, directed movement of water that flows in one of the Earth's oceans."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ocean_current"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ocean current","blocks":[{"text":"An ocean current is a continuous, directed movement of seawater generated by a number of forces acting upon the water, including wind, the Coriolis effect, breaking waves, cabbeling, and temperature and salinity differences. Depth contours, shoreline configurations, and interactions with other currents influence a current's direction and strength. Ocean currents move both horizontally, on scales that can span entire oceans, as well as vertically, with vertical currents playing an important role in the movement of nutrients and gases, such as carbon dioxide, between the surface and the deep ocean."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/Corrientes-oceanicas.png/330px-Corrientes-oceanicas.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ocean_current","wikidata":"Q129558"},{"title":"Ocean","blocks":[{"text":"The ocean is the body of salt water that covers approximately 70.8% of Earth. The ocean is conventionally divided into large bodies of water, which are also referred to as oceans, and are themselves mostly divided into seas, gulfs, and subsequent bodies of water. The ocean contains 97% of Earth's water and is the primary component of Earth's hydrosphere, acting as a huge reservoir of heat for Earth's energy budget, as well as for its carbon cycle and water cycle, forming the basis for climate and weather patterns worldwide. The ocean is essential to life on Earth, harboring most of Earth's animals and protist life. It originated photosynthesis and therefore Earth's atmospheric oxygen, and still supplies half of it."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Pacific_Ocean_as_viewed_from_GOES-18_on_September_23%2C_2023.jpg/330px-Pacific_Ocean_as_viewed_from_GOES-18_on_September_23%2C_2023.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ocean","wikidata":"Q9430"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/CSIRO_ScienceImage_11128_The_bathymetry_of_the_Kerguelen_Plateau_in_the_Southern_Ocean_governs_the_course_of_the_new_current_part_of_the_global_network_of_ocean_currents.jpg/500px-thumbnail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/CSIRO_ScienceImage_11128_The_bathymetry_of_the_Kerguelen_Plateau_in_the_Southern_Ocean_governs_the_course_of_the_new_current_part_of_the_global_network_of_ocean_currents.jpg/1280px-thumbnail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/CSIRO_ScienceImage_11128_The_bathymetry_of_the_Kerguelen_Plateau_in_the_Southern_Ocean_governs_the_course_of_the_new_current_part_of_the_global_network_of_ocean_currents.jpg/500px-thumbnail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The bathymetry of the Kerguelen Plateau in the Southern Ocean governs the course of the Kerguelen deep western boundary current, part of the global network of ocean currents.[3][4]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACSIRO_ScienceImage_11128_The_bathymetry_of_the_Kerguelen_Plateau_in_the_Southern_Ocean_governs_the_course_of_the_new_current_part_of_the_global_network_of_ocean_currents.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Meddes-20060320-browse.jpg/500px-Meddes-20060320-browse.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Meddes-20060320-browse.jpg/500px-Meddes-20060320-browse.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Coupling data collected by NASA/JPL by several different satellite-borne sensors, researchers have been able to \"break through\" the ocean's surface to detect \"Meddies\" – super-salty warm-water eddies that originate in the Mediterranean Sea and then sink more than a half-mile underwater in the Atlantic Ocean. The Meddies are shown in red in this scientific figure.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMeddes-20060320-browse.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Marine_microplankton.jpg/500px-Marine_microplankton.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Marine_microplankton.jpg/1280px-Marine_microplankton.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Marine_microplankton.jpg/500px-Marine_microplankton.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Plankton are dispersed by ocean currents.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMarine_microplankton.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Fig_1.4.1_Atmospheric_circulation_cells%2C_dominant_wind_directions.png/500px-Fig_1.4.1_Atmospheric_circulation_cells%2C_dominant_wind_directions.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Fig_1.4.1_Atmospheric_circulation_cells%2C_dominant_wind_directions.png/500px-Fig_1.4.1_Atmospheric_circulation_cells%2C_dominant_wind_directions.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Human-induced climate change is leading to long-term alterations in ocean and atmospheric circulation. The accumulation of greenhouse gases traps extra heat within the Earth's system, causing both the atmosphere and oceans to warm. Notably, over 90% of this trapped heat is absorbed by the oceans. There are signs that crucial circulation patterns are shifting, with growing evidence suggesting that the Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation may be slowing down.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFig_1.4.1_Atmospheric_circulation_cells%2C_dominant_wind_directions.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Skipjack_tuna_transporting_at_Pondokdadap_Fishingport.jpg/500px-Skipjack_tuna_transporting_at_Pondokdadap_Fishingport.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Skipjack_tuna_transporting_at_Pondokdadap_Fishingport.jpg/1280px-Skipjack_tuna_transporting_at_Pondokdadap_Fishingport.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Skipjack_tuna_transporting_at_Pondokdadap_Fishingport.jpg/500px-Skipjack_tuna_transporting_at_Pondokdadap_Fishingport.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Skipjack tuna fishery in Indonesia.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASkipjack_tuna_transporting_at_Pondokdadap_Fishingport.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Ocean_currents_1943_%28borderless%293.png/500px-Ocean_currents_1943_%28borderless%293.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Ocean_currents_1943_%28borderless%293.png/1280px-Ocean_currents_1943_%28borderless%293.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Ocean_currents_1943_%28borderless%293.png/500px-Ocean_currents_1943_%28borderless%293.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A 1943 map of the world's ocean currents","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOcean_currents_1943_(borderless)3.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/Recording_Current_Meter.jpg/500px-Recording_Current_Meter.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/Recording_Current_Meter.jpg/500px-Recording_Current_Meter.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A recording current meter. It records information about currents (speed, direction, depth, temperature).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARecording_Current_Meter.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Mid-ocean_ridge_system.gif/500px-Mid-ocean_ridge_system.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Mid-ocean_ridge_system.gif/500px-Mid-ocean_ridge_system.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":" Bathymetry of the ocean floor showing the continental shelves and oceanic plateaus (red), the mid-ocean ridges (yellow-green) and the abyssal plains (blue to purple)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMid-ocean_ridge_system.gif"}],"sounds":{"ocean current":[{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6e/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Flame%2C_not_lame-ocean_current.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/6/6e/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Flame%2C_not_lame-ocean_current.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Flame%2C_not_lame-ocean_current.wav.mp3"},{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/tr